	  --> 3. NOTE: YOU WILL GET AWS RDS PostgreSQL Connection Timeout 

			1. Problem
			- Unable to connect to AWS RDS PostgreSQL database.
			- Error: Connection timeout expired
			- Endpoint: database-1.czy6we00s6m8.eu-north-1.rds.amazonaws.com
			- Port: 5432
			- AWS Region: Europe (Stockholm), eu-north-1

			2. Root Cause
			The RDS instance was using the DEFAULT SECURITY GROUP, which allowed inbound traffic only from resources associated with the same security group.
			My local computer was not covered by this rule, so the connection was blocked.

			3. Security Group Configuration Added- MyDatabaseServerGroup 

			| Setting | Value |
			| Security Group | sg-09177843777c53313 |
			| Type | PostgreSQL |
			| Protocol | TCP |
			| Port | 5432 |
			| Source | My IP (Public-IP/32) |
			
			
			
-->			My IP- matlb woh same internet connection jo AWS use kar rha hai uska Network Use karlo aur mera Computer aur AWS dono same network use
                   kar rhe toh connection ban gya dubara phirse issue aaya toh same MY IP select karke Save karna hoga security group me tab connection banega.
            YEH CONNECTION ALLOW KAR RAHA SAME NETWORK KE IP KO DATABASE SE CONNECT KARNE KE LIYE.		

-->         In place of MY IP- agar anywhere-IPv4 kardiya toh har system IPv4 wala connect kar paayega, same anywhere-IPV6	kardiya toh har system IPv6 wala
            connect kar paayega toh security group bahut important topic hai check this.	

			Why did I add this rule?

			- To allow my computer to connect to the RDS database.
			- Port 5432 is the default PostgreSQL port.
			- Selecting **My IP** restricts access to my current public IP address.

			4. How the Connection Works

			My Computer → Internet → AWS Security Group → RDS PostgreSQL (Port 5432)
			The security group checks whether incoming traffic matches its inbound rules before allowing access to the database.

			5. How I Verified the Fix
            
			Run this command in PowerShell:
			powershell: Test-NetConnection database-1.czy6we00s6m8.eu-north-1.rds.amazonaws.com -Port 5432
			Expected result: TcpTestSucceeded : True

			6. Key Learnings
			- A connection timeout can indicate a network or firewall configuration issue.
			- Security groups act as virtual firewalls for AWS resources.
			- The database region (Stockholm) and my physical location (Mumbai) were not the root cause.
			- If my public IP changes, I may need to update the security group rule.
			- Avoid `0.0.0.0/0` for database access because it allows connections from any IPv4 address.

			Conclusion: The connection started working after I added an inbound PostgreSQL rule allowing traffic from my public IP on TCP port `5432`.
			
			

